          "source_text": "<STRUCT_0001>\nChoose a geometric point <MATH_0001> of <MATH_0002>.\nVia the equivalence of\nLemma <REF_0001>\nthe sheaf <MATH_0003> corresponds to a finite <MATH_0004>-module\n<MATH_0005> with a continuous <MATH_0006>-action.\nLet <MATH_0007> be the image of the homomorphism\n<MATH_0008> giving the action.\nObserve that <MATH_0009> is finite as <MATH_0010> is a finite <MATH_0011>-module\n(see proof of Lemma <REF_0002>).\nThe surjective continuous homomorphism\n<MATH_0012>\ncorresponds to a Galois object <MATH_0013> of\n<MATH_0014> with automorphism group <MATH_0015>, see\nFundamental Groups, Section <REF_0003>.\nLet <MATH_0016> be an <MATH_0017>-Sylow subgroup.\nWe claim that <MATH_0018> works. Namely, let <MATH_0019>\nbe a geometric point over <MATH_0020>. The image of\n<MATH_0021>\nis <MATH_0022> as follows from the functorial\nnature of fundamental groups. Hence the action of <MATH_0023>\non <MATH_0024> corresponding to <MATH_0025> is through\nthe map <MATH_0026>, see\nRemark <REF_0004>.\nLet <MATH_0027>\nbe as in Lemma <REF_0005>.\nThis induces a filtration\n<MATH_0028>\nsuch that the successive quotients are constant with\nvalue <MATH_0029>.\nFinally, the degree of <MATH_0030> is prime to <MATH_0031>\nas it is equal to the index of <MATH_0032> in <MATH_0033>.\n<STRUCT_0002>",
